  "description": "Compute multi-head scaled dot-product self-attention purely using vectorized NumPy operations.\n\nNumPy broadcasting and tensor transposition implement multi-head projections without requiring explicit Python loops.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{O} = \\text{Concat}(\\text{Softmax}(\\mathbf{Q}_h \\mathbf{K}_h^T / \\sqrt{d_k}) \\mathbf{V}_h)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Partition input tensor into parallel head subspaces along dimension axis\n2. Evaluate scaled dot-product attention scores per head\n3. Apply numerically stabilized softmax along key dimension and recombine heads\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list of shape (N, D) rounded to 4 decimal places.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Multi-Head Attention\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNeural networks achieve their remarkable expressive power by stacking linear layers interspersed with non-linear activation functions and normalization layers. Without non-linear activations, any deep network would collapse into a single shallow linear regression. **Multi-Head Attention** ensures signals propagate cleanly through dozens or hundreds of layers without vanishing or exploding.\n\nNumPy broadcasting and tensor transposition implement multi-head projections without requiring explicit Python loops. In this problem, the goal is to: compute multi-head scaled dot-product self-attention purely using vectorized numpy operations.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{O} = \\text{Concat}(\\text{Softmax}(\\mathbf{Q}_h \\mathbf{K}_h^T / \\sqrt{d_k}) \\mathbf{V}_h)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Partition input tensor into parallel head subspaces along dimension axis\n2. **Phase 2**: Evaluate scaled dot-product attention scores per head\n3. **Phase 3**: Apply numerically stabilized softmax along key dimension and recombine heads\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Gradient Flow**: Carefully designed activation functions (like ReLU, GELU, and Swish) maintain strong gradient signals during backpropagation.\n* **Internal Covariate Shift**: Normalization techniques (like LayerNorm and RMSNorm) standardize hidden states across channels or batch dimensions, allowing models to train stably at higher learning rates.\n* **Hardware Friendly**: Modern deep learning frameworks implement these layers using fused GPU kernels to reduce memory round-trips.",
